<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khanate of Kazan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1438–<a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Kazan Khanate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> state on the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, in the lands of the former Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, with its capital at Kazan. One of the successor states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, it is dated by historians either to 1437 or 1438, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a> took power in Kazan, or to 1445, when his son Maxmud began to rule there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> was the state religion, and real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, an assembly of the highest aristocracy. From 1487 to 1521 it was in effect a Muscovite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. The forces of Ivan the Terrible took Kazan in 1552 after a siege of more than 40 days, and the population was massacred.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> stood in the former territory of Volga Bulgaria, which may have regained a degree of independence within the disintegrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> by the turn of the 15th century. Historians disagree about its founding. One view dates it from 1437 or 1438, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a>, a former khan of the Golden Horde, took power in Kazan with the support of the local nobility. Most sources, according to the Great Russian Encyclopedia, support autumn 1445, when his son Maxmud began to rule. The History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> concludes that no version is confirmed by two independent sources, and no document of the khanate survived the Russian conquest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1445 Maxmud and his brother Yakub defeated the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II of Moscow</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and captured him, and Vasily agreed to pay a ransom and annual tribute. The khanate was prone to civil turmoil, with khans enthroned and deposed at assemblies of the nobility. In July 1487 Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and placed Möxämmädämin on the throne; during his reign of 1487–1495 the khanate was in effect a Muscovite protectorate. In 1521 the Crimean prince Sahib Giray took the throne and led a successful campaign against Moscow.</p><p>The growth of Crimean influence displeased pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> nobles, who deposed Safa Giray in the uprising of 1545–1546, but he returned with Nogai troops. After his death in 1549 his two-year-old son Ütämeşgäräy became khan, with his mother Söyembikä as regent. In 1551 the nobility accepted Russian terms, and in 1552 an uprising expelled the Russian garrison.</p><p>That year forces of Ivan the Terrible, operating from the fortress of Sviyazhsk, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. After more than 40 days they blew up the walls and stormed the city on 2 October 1552 (Old Style). The khan Yadegar Mokhammad was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>; most defenders were killed and the population was massacred. Resistance continued in the countryside, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> stopped resisting in May 1557.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The population included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, Chuvash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mordvins">Mordvins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, and the official language was Volga Turki, or Old Tatar. Sunni Islam of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> was the state religion. The khan was formally sole ruler, but real power belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, an assembly of the highest religious and secular aristocracy. Towns lived by crafts and by trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and the Taşayaq fair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> was the main fair of the Volga region. Captives were held as slaves and exported.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khanate of Kazan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Khanate of Kazan</b> emerged around 1438, the Crimean Khanate under Hacı Giray around 1441 and Astrakhan around 1466, while the remaining core became known as the Great Horde.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasily III (r. 1505–1533) completed his father's work, ending the independence of Pskov in 1510, taking Smolensk from Lithuania in 1514 and absorbing the last Ryazan principality around 1521, while Moscow repeatedly intervened in the succession of the <b>Khanate of Kazan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongol conquest the region remained a centre of Islamic culture under the Golden Horde and later the <b>Khanate of Kazan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
